= 1
{parent=Paper 18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Sending a matrix to its last column gives the <special orthogonal sphere fibration>
$$
SO(n-1)\longrightarrow SO(n)\longrightarrow S^{n-1},\qquad n\geq2.
$$
The action is transitive and the stabilizer of that column is $SO(n-1)$. Local orthonormal frames, obtained by <Gram-Schmidt process> near a chosen unit vector, give local sections and hence local bundle trivializations. This allows the <long exact sequence of homotopy groups of a fibration> to be applied.

At the bottom of the induction, $SO(1)$ is a point and $SO(2)$ is the rotation circle $S^1$. For $SO(3)$, identify $\mathbb R^3$ with imaginary <quaternions>. A <unit quaternion> acts by $v\mapsto qvq^{-1}$, preserving length and orientation. Every rotation through angle $\theta$ about a unit imaginary axis $u$ comes from $q=\cos(\theta/2)+u\sin(\theta/2)$. The kernel is precisely $\{1,-1\}$, since a <quaternion> commuting with all imaginary <quaternions> is real. Thus this gives the twofold <covering space> $S^3\to SO(3)$. Its simply connected total space is the <universal cover>, yielding $\pi_1SO(3)=\mathbb Z/2$. Coverings induce isomorphisms on higher <homotopy groups>, so $\pi_2SO(3)=\pi_2S^3=0$ and $\pi_3SO(3)=\pi_3S^3=\mathbb Z$.

For $n\geq4$, both $\pi_1S^{n-1}$ and $\pi_2S^{n-1}$ vanish. The <Serre fibration> sequence therefore identifies $\pi_1SO(n-1)$ with $\pi_1SO(n)$ and propagates vanishing of $\pi_2$. Path-connectedness also propagates from connected fiber and base. Consequently the full low-degree answer is
$$
\boxed{\pi_0SO(n)=\{*\},\quad\pi_2SO(n)=0\quad(n\geq1),\qquad
\pi_1SO(n)=\begin{cases}0,&n=1,\\\mathbb Z,&n=2,\\\mathbb Z/2,&n\geq3.\end{cases}}
$$
Here $\pi_0$ records the single connected component.

For the remaining third group use $SO(3)\to SO(4)\to S^3$. The relevant exact segment, using the supplied fourth <homotopy group>, is
$$
\mathbb Z/2\longrightarrow\mathbb Z\longrightarrow\pi_3SO(4)\longrightarrow\mathbb Z\longrightarrow0.
$$
The first homomorphism is zero since a <torsion group> cannot map nontrivially to $\mathbb Z$. Thus it reduces to a <short exact sequence> $0\to\mathbb Z\to\pi_3SO(4)\to\mathbb Z\to0$. These groups are abelian and the final free group has a lift of its generator, so the sequence splits. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\pi_3SO(1)=\pi_3SO(2)=0,\qquad\pi_3SO(3)=\mathbb Z,\qquad\pi_3SO(4)=\mathbb Z\oplus\mathbb Z.}
$$
These are the <low homotopy groups of special orthogonal groups>.

= 2
{parent=Paper 18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

An <immersion> $M^4\to\mathbb R^5$ would inject $TM$ into the trivial rank-five bundle. Taking its orthogonal complement gives a normal <real line bundle> $\nu$ with $TM\oplus\nu\cong\varepsilon^5$. The <Whitney product formula for Stiefel–Whitney classes> requires
$$
w(TM)w(\nu)=1.
$$
The <real line bundle> need not be orientable, but it has no <Stiefel–Whitney classes> above degree one.

Let $a,b$ be the pulled-back degree-one generators from the two real projective planes. Their mod-two <cohomology ring>, by the <Künneth theorem>, is $\mathbb F_2[a,b]/(a^3,b^3)$. To compute the tangent classes, at a line $L$ the projective tangent space is $\operatorname{Hom}(L,L^\perp)$. Adding $\operatorname{Hom}(L,L)\cong\mathbb R$ gives $\operatorname{Hom}(L,\mathbb R^3)\cong3L$, with the real line identified with its dual using a metric. Thus the <stabilized tangent bundle of real projective space> gives $T\mathbb{RP}^2\oplus\varepsilon^1\cong3\gamma$ and
$$
w(T\mathbb{RP}^2)=(1+a)^3=1+a+a^2.
$$
The <tangent bundle> of the product is the direct sum of the pulled-back tangent bundles. Since $(1+a+a^2)(1+a)=1$ in this truncated ring, and similarly for $b$,
$$
w(TM)^{-1}=(1+a)(1+b)=1+a+b+ab.
$$
The degree-two class $ab$ is nonzero by the product-ring basis. It cannot be a class of the rank-one bundle $\nu$. Equivalently $w_1(TM)^2+w_2(TM)=ab\ne0$, violating the <codimension-one Stiefel–Whitney immersion obstruction>. Hence **$\mathbb{RP}^2\times\mathbb{RP}^2$ admits no <immersion> in $\mathbb R^5$**.

= 3
{parent=Paper 18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Apply the <splitting principle for complex vector bundles> simultaneously to $E$ and $F$. This can be implemented by successive projective bundle pullbacks; the projective bundle theorem makes their composite pullback injective on integral <cohomology>. After pullback write $E=L_1\oplus L_2$ and $F=M_1\oplus M_2$, with <Chern roots> $x_1,x_2$ and $y_1,y_2$. Let
$$
A=x_1+x_2=c_1(E),\quad C=x_1x_2=c_2(E),\qquad
B=y_1+y_2=c_1(F),\quad D=y_1y_2=c_2(F).
$$
Since the <first Chern class> of a tensor product of <complex line bundles> is the sum of their first <Chern classes>, the four roots of $E\otimes F$ are $x_i+y_j$, $1\leq i,j\leq2$.

Compute their six pairwise products integrally. The two pairs within a fixed $x_i$ row contribute
$$
\sum_{i=1}^2(x_i+y_1)(x_i+y_2)=x_1^2+x_2^2+AB+2D=A^2-2C+AB+2D.
$$
The four pairs between the two rows contribute
$$
\sum_{j,k=1}^2(x_1+y_j)(x_2+y_k)=4C+2AB+B^2.
$$
Their sum is the second elementary symmetric <polynomial> of the tensor roots. Injectivity of the pullback returns the resulting identity to $X$, giving
$$
\boxed{c_2(E\otimes F)=c_1(E)^2+3c_1(E)c_1(F)+c_1(F)^2+2c_2(E)+2c_2(F).}
$$
Products here are <cup products> in integral <cohomology>. This <second Chern class of a tensor product of rank-two bundles> formula uses no division by two and therefore holds without assuming the integral <cohomology> of $X$ is torsion-free.

